Right—the paradox is that doing nothing together usually has to be built by doing quite a lot first.

You don’t really “get” a girl like an objective; you create the conditions for mutual interest: approach, flirt, invite, listen, show up, make plans, take some risk, be interesting, be kind, respect her signals. If she reciprocates, eventually the relationship accumulates enough trust that neither person has to keep performing.

Then “nothing” changes meaning.

At first:

\text{silence} \approx \text{awkward uncertainty}

Later:

\text{silence} \approx \text{comfort + trust + presence}

So Frog and Toad doing nothing together is actually a pretty advanced social state. The absence of activity is carrying information: I’m comfortable enough with you that I don’t need to entertain you, impress you, or fill every second.

You usually act your way into the ability to be still together.

And that generalizes beyond romance: competence buys ease, conversation buys comfortable silence, work buys rest, trust buys simplicity.

